Hi Karl,

Thanks for your quick reply!
Then it makes sense indeed.

I somehow always associated an additive effect of QTLs with that the effect of the QTLs on the phenotypes "add up". So in the case of the effectplot: that RILs with the BB allele of both QTLs display a lower value than RILs with of only one of the QTL the BB allele. But that doesnt seem to be the case, as the errorbars overlap and the ANOVA doesn't give a significant difference for RILs wit only one BB allele and RILs with of both QTLs the BB allele.

But if understand you correctly, for the results from scantwo, an additive effect solely means that there is no epistasis/ the effect of one QTL is not dependent on the genotype at another QTL?

Thanks!

Jasper

Op donderdag 7 mei 2020 20:59:42 UTC+2 schreef Karl Broman:

> It sounds to me like the results all support the conclusion: there are three QTL, and they act additively (no epistasis).
>
> To say QTL act additively means the effect of one QTL is not dependent on the genotype at another. This corresponds to having parallel lines in the effect plot.
>
> I find scantwo most useful for identifying potential interactions among QTL, and for determining if there may be multiple QTL on a single chromosome. The results you're getting say that adjusting for the locus on chr.A, there's evidence for a QTL on chr.B, and vice versa.
>
> karl
